`dev/ADJUDICATION-PREREQS.md` §6b designed this round from the two rounds
already on disk. This is what happened when the design met the runs it would
have to be drawn from. Three things changed, one of them a refusal in
`pf_round_sample()` firing on real data exactly as intended, and one of them a
regression this work put in and the tests took out again.

## 1. What is on disk, and what each run can support

Every run was opened and its shape read rather than inferred from its name.

| run | built | shape | identities / grants | MAYBE | pairs | OBJECTID |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `TRI-V2-MATCH.rds` | 10-25 | `list(status, grants)` | 91,690 / 596,539 | **24,107** idents | **none** | **yes** |
| `PF-MATCH-RESULT-50k.rds` | 08-19 | `pf_match` | — / 50,000 | 10,898 | 3,372,855 | no |
| `PF-MATCH-RESULT-1k.rds` | 08-20 | `pf_match` | — / 5,000 | 1,006 | 312,468 | no |
| `TRI-PILOT-MATCH.rds` | 10-23 | `list(status, grants)` | — | — | none | — |
| `FILER-SAMPLE-MATCH.rds` | 10-21 | `list(status, grants)` | — | — | none | — |

Two facts fall out of that table and they pull in opposite directions.

**The two pair-complete runs are the wrong grant vintage.** Both carry the
archived GT extract schema — `SIGOCPYAMOUN`, `FILEREIN`, `TAXPEREND` — and no
`OBJECTID`, so neither can be fingerprinted. §8 of the prereqs records that
names agree on **0%** of `grant_id`s joined across the two known vintages, so
labels drawn against them could never be re-attached to a production grant.
They are dry-run frames, nothing more.

**The production run is the right vintage and has no pairs.** `TRI-V2` is
serialized as the identity-grain status frame beside the grant-grain source,
with no `$result` and no `pairs` attribute. It fingerprints to
`cb3c78ff0f1a9546`.

The two elements are keyed differently — `R<k>` in the status frame against
`recipient_uid == k` in the grants — and share no id, which at first reading
looks like the cross-vintage disagreement again. It is not: it is one
population at two grains. Verified on 500 sampled identities, the identity's
`recip_name` appears among that `uid`'s grant names **500/500** and the tier
agrees **500/500**. `dev/draw-round-stage1.R` asserts both before it draws.

## 2. `pf_status()` already carries the case features

The draw needs the pass that produced the pick, the margin to the runner-up and
`n_close`. `pf_round_sample()` dug all three out of the pairs. `pf_status()`
returns all three as columns. On all **5,000** rows of the 1k run, every tier:

| column | agreement |
|---|---|
| `match_pass` | 0 disagreements, 0 NA mismatches |
| `n_close` | 0 disagreements, 0 NA mismatches |
| `margin` | max absolute difference **5e-04** |

The margin gap is `pf_status()` rounding to three decimals. Immaterial to a
band, not to a threshold, so a `pf_match` keeps the result frame's unrounded
value and a status frame gets the rounded one. Documented on the parameter.

Reading the features off the status frame instead of the pairs is what lets the
production run be drawn from at all. It also **put a regression in**: the
fixture's result frame has no `pass` column, the pass lives only in its pairs,
and the simplified code took `NA` for all 60 cases — one stratum reported as
several. `test-round.R` failed on it. The pairs fallback is back, and the
missing refusal it exposed is now a stop: **a `by` column that is present but
entirely `NA`** is an unstratified draw that reports strata, same as an absent
one.

## 3. §6b.3's strata are too fine, at both scales

§6b.3 asks for `tier · pass · purpose · margin band · dollar band`. Measured:

**The 1,006-case dry-run frame**, cells and how many are starved at n=400:

| design | cells | n<5 | n<20 | share<1 | share<2 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `tier · pass` | 7 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| `tier · pass · purpose` | 14 | 0 | 5 | 0 | 0 |
| `tier · pass · margin` | 21 | 2 | 10 | 0 | 2 |
| `tier · pass · purpose · margin` | 42 | 11 | 25 | 5 | 15 |
| **§6b.3 as written** | **141** | 74 | 130 | **54** | 83 |

**The 24,107-identity production frame**, same question at n=400:

| design | cells | n<20 | share<1 | share<2 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `tier · pass` | 10 | 2 | 2 | 2 |
| `tier · pass · purpose` | 20 | 4 | 4 | 6 |
| `tier · pass · purpose · margin` | 56 | 11 | 16 | 23 |
| **§6b.3 plus dollar** | **206** | 54 | 120 | **159** |

A 400-case draw over 206 cells in which 120 receive less than one case is not
a stratified sample. Enlarging the frame does not fix it — the production frame
is 24x the dry-run frame and the full cross gets *worse*, because more passes
exist to cross against.

**Stage 1 stratifies on `tier · pass · purpose`.** The margin and dollar bands
are **recorded on every drawn case** instead, which is what they are actually
needed for: reading the realized distribution, and topping up thin cells in
stage 2 once there is something to top up from.

## 4. The two T8 flags, measured

Neither flag exists in code — `ADJUDICATION-PLAN.md` §7 step 4 is unbuilt — so
both were computed here on the 1,006-case dry-run frame. `flag_arm_shape` from
`pf_is_arm()` and `pf_arm_core()` on pick against runner-up; `flag_ntee_support`
from `ntee_common_code` in `data-dev/BMF-AUGMENTED-unified-fixed.csv`, support
being `11` / `12` / `19`, joined on all 1,006 picks and all 969 runners-up with
no misses.

| flag | population | of frame | §6b.3 floor |
|---|---|---|---|
| `flag_arm_shape` | **156** | 15.5% | 100 — **met** |
| `flag_ntee_support` | **60** | 6.0% | 100 — **short** |

They overlap on 31 cases. The flagged arm-shape pairs are the shape the plan
describes: `CHRIST THE KING SCHOOL FOUNDATION` against `CHRIST THE KING
SCHOOL`, `CENTRA HEALTH FOUNDATION` against `CENTRA HEALTH`. Of the 60
NTEE-support picks, 50 are code `11`, 9 are `12`, 1 is `19`.

Asked for a floor of 100 on a population of 60, `pf_round_sample()` stops and
says why:

    `flag_ntee_support` has 60 cases in the frame but a floor of 100 was asked
    for. At 60 the Wilson interval is about 24 points wide, which is why the
    floor exists; a short cell cannot carry the finding.

That refusal was written against a synthetic fixture last night. This is it
firing on the real frame, and it is correct to fire: at n=60 and p=.60 the
interval is 0.47–0.71, against 0.50–0.69 at 100 and 0.52–0.68 at 156. T8 exists
because 60% on n=15 cannot carry a decision; 60% on n=60 cannot either.

Scaled to the production tier the rate would put `flag_ntee_support` near 1,400
cases, comfortably past the floor — but that is an extrapolation of a GT-vintage
rate, not a measurement, and it cannot be checked, which is §5.

## 5. The blocker, stated precisely

**Both flags compare the pick to the runner-up. Nothing else in the design
does.** The strata, the bands, the fingerprint and the dollar totals all come
off the status and grants frames. So:

* the production run supports the whole design **except** the two flags;
* the pair-complete runs support the flags and **cannot be fingerprinted**;
* no run on disk supports both.

The unblocker is one serialization, not a re-design: re-run or re-save the
production cascade keeping `$result` with its `pairs` attribute. Then
`pf_round_sample()` takes it as a `pf_match`, the flags are computable, and the
floors are reachable. Until then T8 waits for stage 2 and stage 1 draws without
flag oversampling — which costs stage 1 nothing, because §6b.2 sizes stage 1
off the per-arm high-confidence precision claim and T8 is a separate cut.

## 6. The draw

    drew 400 of 24,107 MAYBE identities into 16 strata (seed 20261102)
    population cb3c78ff0f1a9546 -- pf_grade_verdicts() will enforce it

| | |
|---|---|
| cases | 400 |
| frame | 24,107 MAYBE identities |
| strata | 16 (8 passes × 2 purpose bands) |
| grant dollars | $66,688,015 |
| median identity | $6,010 |
| purpose / bare | 227 / 173 |
| margin tie / narrow / clear | 148 / 123 / 129 |
| dollar q1–q4 | 102 / 106 / 99 / 93 |

**Two of the ten passes are absent, and no round size fixes that.** The MAYBE
tier holds **11** `A4-dba-name` identities and **3** `D2-prefix` identities in
total. Their proportional share of 400 is 0.18 and 0.05. They are absent from
stage 1 because they barely exist, not because 400 is too small, and a per-pass
accuracy claim for either is not available at any round size.

The eight that are present, with their share of the tier:

| pass | identities | % of tier | drawn |
|---|---|---|---|
| `B1-token-state` | 13,198 | 54.8% | 219 |
| `C1-name-any` | 3,035 | 12.6% | 50 |
| `A1-name-zip5` | 2,947 | 12.2% | 49 |
| `B2-token-concat` | 1,521 | 6.3% | 26 |
| `C2-dba-any` | 1,384 | 5.7% | 23 |
| `A2-name-state` | 1,373 | 5.7% | 23 |
| `D1-acronym` | 440 | 1.8% | 7 |
| `A3-name-dba` | 195 | 0.8% | 3 |

Proportional allocation puts 219 of 400 in one pass, which is right for
estimating the tier and wrong for comparing passes: §6b.2 wants 250–300 per arm
and this gives one arm 219 and the next 50. **Stage 1 is not sized to compare
passes.** It reads the realized confidence and evidence-class distribution,
which is what §6b.1c says it is for, and the per-arm comparison is stage 2's
job with floors set from what stage 1 shows.

## 7. What is now true

* The round has a frame: 400 cases, fingerprinted, reproducible from the seed
  and the script, tracked in the repository because the labels will key to it.
* It is **not labelled**. No code produces the labels, which is the whole point
  of T1.
* The flags are measured on the dry-run frame and unavailable on the production
  one, pending one re-save.
* `pf_round_sample()` gained a status-frame input, a required fingerprint, an
  all-NA refusal and realized oversample counts, all four because drawing a
  real frame demanded them.
